# Broadcast Networking

The ingest farm handles broadcast network traffic.

Diagnose network problems from the bottom up.

## Troubleshooting Order

When an ingest source cannot be received, consider:

    NIC/link
      |
    interface/VLAN
      |
    IP addressing
      |
    routing
      |
    multicast membership
      |
    multicast forwarding
      |
    UDP socket
      |
    RTP
      |
    MPEG-TS
      |
    GStreamer

Do not immediately modify the GStreamer pipeline when the problem may be
below the application layer.

## Multicast

For multicast ingest consider:

- multicast group
- source address
- destination port
- receiving interface
- routing table
- IGMP version
- IGMP membership
- SSM vs ASM
- upstream multicast forwarding
- Docker networking
- socket binding

Do not assume that joining a multicast group guarantees packets will arrive.

## IGMP

When using multicast, distinguish:

- IGMP membership
- multicast routing
- multicast forwarding
- application socket membership

A successful IGMP join does not prove that media packets are arriving.

## UDP

Consider:

- receive buffer size
- socket binding
- port conflicts
- packet rate
- packet loss
- interface selection
- MTU where relevant

Do not increase socket buffers blindly. Explain why the change is required.

## RTP

When RTP is involved, distinguish:

- UDP packet loss
- RTP sequence-number loss
- RTP reordering
- RTP jitter
- timestamp discontinuities

Do not describe all of these as "packet loss".

## Diagnostics

Prefer evidence-producing diagnostics such as:

    ip addr
    ip route
    ip maddr
    ss
    tcpdump
    ethtool
    gst-inspect-1.0

Use packet captures when they can establish whether the problem exists before
the application.

## Docker

When diagnosing multicast inside containers, distinguish host networking
behaviour from container networking behaviour.

Do not assume that a pipeline working directly on the host will work
unchanged inside a container.

## Safety

Do not make destructive network changes without understanding their scope.

Do not flush routing tables, firewall rules or network configuration merely
as a diagnostic shortcut.